            <ArticleTitle>A Case of Benign Phyllodes Tumor in a Transgender Woman Receiving Cross-Sex Hormones.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1066896917743011</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/1066896917743011</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Phyllodes tumor is a relatively uncommon fibroepithelial neoplasm of the breast characterized by proliferation of both stromal and epithelial elements. Benign phyllodes tumors are distinguished from fibroadenomas by their prominent leaf-like architecture and exaggerated intracanalicular stromal growth pattern. Typically, these lesions affect older natal females; however, we present what we believe is the first reported case of benign phyllodes tumor in a hormonally treated transgender woman.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
